Subject: Scope element snapshots to the transport that read them

Upstream keeps one element snapshot per window for every caller, so any
client's get_window_state tree read retires every other client's element
tokens for that window. Key snapshots by owner instead: a retained
transport (a session_begin connection that stays open) owns the snapshots
its calls read and resolves only its own, and another owner's token is
refused as foreign_element_token; calls without one share a lane as before.
Each owner keeps at most 8 snapshots per process and 32 in all, at most 256
owners hold snapshots, all of them together hold at most 262,144 elements
(the least recently publishing owner's oldest snapshot goes first), and a
transport's snapshots are released when its control connection closes.
Snapshot numbers start at a random point in each process and the process
knows the range it has issued, so a token a previous daemon minted is
refused as stale instead of naming one of this daemon's snapshots. A stale
refusal keeps its message and adds why, when known: the caller's own newer
read replaced the snapshot, a bound dropped it, a later read could not
scope the window, or an earlier driver process issued the token (each
owner remembers its last 64 retirements). The daemon advertises it as
metadata.snapshot_scope = "transport", and the operator sessions_list
reply counts each owner's snapshots.

Applies to cua-driver-rs-v0.28.2 (fc188250) after Mako's release.patch
+24, whose retained transports (TransportSessionLease) it builds on.

